---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '~/components/Section.astro';
import Button from '~/components/Button.astro';
import PrintMasthead from '~/components/PrintMasthead.astro';
/* The sheet stylesheet — the page box, the print type scale, the panel grid,
 * the band treatments. Shared with /app; only what is specific to this page's
 * art is left in the block at the bottom of this file. */
import '~/styles/one-pager.css';

/**
 * PANTRY POINT — /pantry-point
 *
 * A rebuild of the printed one-pager in `designs/smsp-one-pager.svg`, the same
 * way `/app` is a rebuild of `designs/sma-one-pager.svg`: the argument is
 * carried over, the Illustrator sheet is not. It is set in Onest with the
 * site's tokens rather than the original's Roboto and its own blues, and it is
 * the source of the PDF — `scripts/build-pdf.mjs` renders this route to
 * `dist/pantry-point.pdf`, so there is one copy of the copy rather than a page
 * and an asset drifting apart.
 *
 * THE RENAME. The product is Pantry Point. It was the Smart Pantry, and the
 * source flyer says so on every line. The old name appears exactly once on this
 * page, in the hero paragraph, and never again: anyone who knew it needs the
 * bridge, and anyone who did not should not have to carry two names through a
 * sheet they are scanning in five seconds. Nothing here is titled "formerly
 * known as" — that is a redirect note, not a headline.
 *
 * TENSE, WHICH IS THE STRUCTURAL DECISION ON THIS PAGE.
 *
 * The original flyer describes one specific mechanism in the present tense —
 * short survey, credits to an account, scan a phone at the machine. That is
 * how the PILOT was run. It is not a claim about how a Pantry Point works
 * today, and writing it as one would put an undated 2019 product description in
 * front of an administrator as though it were an offer.
 *
 * So the page splits along that seam:
 *
 *   - The DURABLE argument — footprint, staffing, hours, the machines it works
 *     with, what it can hold, and why it does not single anybody out — is in
 *     the present tense, because it is a property of the model rather than of
 *     one deployment.
 *   - The MECHANISM is in its own band, in the past tense, labelled as the
 *     pilot. It earns its place: it is the part that was actually tested, and a
 *     campus starting from scratch has to decide the same things.
 *
 * The data argument — the flyer's "DATA DRIVEN" panel — moved into the pilot
 * band rather than staying a feature panel, and for the same reason. The two
 * points of contact it describes are the survey and the scan, so it is a
 * property of that mechanism and cannot be promised independently of it.
 *
 * COPY NOTES.
 *
 * The flyer's personas, Evette and Niamh, are gone. Named personas work in the
 * present tense, which is how `/app` still uses them; recounting a finished
 * pilot through invented people reads as though they were participants. The
 * two paths matter and are kept — one route to credits was a survey, the other
 * was a purchase — but as routes rather than as characters.
 *
 * "Food insecure" as a label on a person is gone with them, per the language
 * rule. It described the reader's situation as an identity, and the sentence
 * does not need it: what mattered was that credits were granted rather than
 * bought.
 *
 * TODO(content): whether any Pantry Point is running anywhere today. This page
 * is deliberately silent on it — there is no "currently at N campuses" line —
 * because that is a fact nobody has confirmed, and a stale number is worse than
 * no number. If the answer is "none right now", the pilot band's framing is
 * already honest and nothing needs to change.
 */

/** Kept in one place: the hero link and the PDF filename both reference it. */
const pdfHref = '/pantry-point.pdf';

/**
 * THE PANELS — five, in two tiers, matching the structure `/app` settled on.
 *
 * The flyer had six blocks: "Solved pain points" (which was three arguments in
 * one box), Modularity, Nutrition, Reduced stigma, Data driven, and the flow
 * diagram. Four of those are feature panels here; the flow diagram has its own
 * band, "Reduced stigma" was promoted out of the grid entirely, and "Data
 * driven" went into the pilot band.
 *
 * WHY "SOLVED PAIN POINTS" WAS SPLIT. It ran floor space, staffing and
 * accessibility together under one heading, which buried the two numbers that
 * do the most work on this page. Space and staffing are the constraints that
 * decide whether a campus can host this at all; accessibility is why it is
 * worth hosting. Those are not the same kind of claim and they should not share
 * a box.
 *
 * WHY TWO TIERS. For the administrator who can say yes, footprint and staffing
 * are the decision-drivers — the rest is supporting argument. Six identical
 * boxes assert that everything matters equally, and it does not.
 *
 * WHY THE HEADINGS ARE SENTENCES. Same trade as `/app`: the flyer's headings
 * were abstract nouns, which make the reader convert a category into a benefit
 * themselves. The heading makes the claim and the body substantiates it. The
 * original nouns are kept in comments so the trade stays visible.
 */

/** What decides whether a campus can host this at all. */
const leadFeatures = [
  {
    // was part of "Solved pain points" — floor space
    title: 'It fits where a food pantry cannot',
    body: [
      // TODO(content): both figures are carried over from the 2019 flyer and
      // are unverified. The machine footprint is checkable against a real unit;
      // the hundred square feet is a claim about somebody else's pantry and
      // should be sourced or softened before this goes in front of a university.
      'A machine takes about six and a half square feet. A staffed pantry needs at least a hundred, plus a door, a store room and somewhere to wait — which is why this fits in a building that has no room for a pantry at all.',
    ],
  },
  {
    // was part of "Solved pain points" — staffing
    title: 'It runs without a volunteer roster',
    body: [
      'Restocking is the job, and it is one you can train someone to do in an afternoon. A staffed pantry needs volunteers, a rota, and someone to train them — and it closes on the days nobody signs up.',
    ],
  },
];

/** The supporting argument: why it is worth hosting once it fits. */
const restFeatures = [
  {
    // was part of "Solved pain points" — accessibility
    title: 'Open at two in the morning',
    body: [
      'It runs around the clock, so a night shift or a gap between classes is not a reason to miss it. Cheap enough to put in several buildings, so it need not mean a walk across campus.',
    ],
  },
  {
    // was "Modularity"
    title: 'It uses the machines already on campus',
    body: [
      'Snack, beverage and refrigerated machines all work. A university decides what to stock and where it goes, rather than taking a fixed unit.',
    ],
  },
  {
    // was "Nutrition"
    title: 'Fresh food, not only what keeps',
    body: [
      'Prepackaged fresh meals, dry goods, shelf-stable items, fruit and vegetables — a wider range than most people expect, and stock that was chosen rather than donated.',
    ],
  },
];

/**
 * THE PILOT FLOW — four captions, in the past tense, sitting under one
 * illustration.
 *
 * They are HTML rather than lettering baked into the art, for the three reasons
 * documented in `public/figures/README.md`: text in an image cannot be selected
 * or translated, does not reflow at 320px, and collapses into a single alt
 * attribute for a screen reader. Which is also why the image is `alt=""` —
 * everything it says is said here.
 */
/* Kept short deliberately. On paper these run as four columns across a 7.5in
 * sheet, so each one is about 28 characters wide — a sentence that reads as one
 * line on screen becomes five in the strip, and the fourth column sets the
 * height of the whole row. Terse also suits the job: the picture is doing the
 * describing and these are labels on its stages, not prose. */
const pilotSteps = [
  'A student who needed credits answered a short survey, and the credits arrived in their account.',
  'A student who wanted to fund the pantry bought credits into their own account instead.',
  'At the machine the phone was the key: scan in, choose, and the credits came off.',
  'Out came oatmeal, salads, nuts, fruit and vegetables — the same stock either way.',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pantry Point"
  description="Pantry Point turns the vending machines a university already has into automated food pantries — open around the clock, and small enough to put in several buildings at once."
>
  <PrintMasthead />

  <Section size="hero">
    <h1 class="text-display-xl font-extrabold text-balance m-0">
      {/* The `&#32;` is load-bearing — see the note on the same line in
          app.astro. Astro's compressor strips source whitespace between tags,
          so without it the accessible name runs together at the line break. */}
      A whole pantry,&#32;<br />
      <span class="text-[var(--color-brand-700)]">in a vending machine.</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="text-lead mt-8 max-w-2xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Pantry Point turns vending machines a university already has into
      automated food pantries, open around the clock. It came out of two years
      of interviews, and was piloted on campus as the Smart Pantry.
    </p>

    {/* Hidden in print: in the PDF this link points at the file you are
        already reading. */}
    <p class="mt-10 m-0 no-print">
      <Button href={pdfHref} variant="secondary">
        Download the Pantry Point one-pager (PDF)
      </Button>
    </p>
  </Section>

  {/* THE DIGNITY BAND.

      This was the flyer's "REDUCED STIGMA" panel, sitting fifth in a grid of six
      between Nutrition and Data driven. It is promoted out of the grid and given
      the loud slot directly after the hero, for the same reason "Featured in"
      was promoted on /app: it is not a product attribute, it is the argument the
      product exists to make, and it read as a weak feature while sitting among
      peers that answered "can we run this".

      It is also the honest headline for the whole page. Floor space and staffing
      are why a university says yes; this is why it matters that they do. Putting
      it before the operational case rather than after says which one is the
      point.

      Inverse, because it is a claim worth making loudly, and because the page
      needs a block of brand colour between the hero and the muted band below.

      NOT wrapped in `.print-row`. That collapses a band into one baseline-aligned
      line, which is right for a strip of logos and wrong for two real paragraphs
      — see the note on the class in one-pager.css. */}
  <Section tone="inverse" size="tight" class="print-band-inverse">
    <h2 class="text-display font-extrabold text-balance m-0">
      Nobody behind you knows who paid.
    </h2>

    {/* ONE paragraph, not the two this started as. Two reasons, and the second
        is the real one:

        `.text-lead` is set to `margin-top: 0` on paper — the screen scale puts
        24px above a lead paragraph, which is right under a headline and wasteful
        between siblings — so a second one here would have printed jammed against
        the first with no leading at all.

        And the half that was cut was the 24-hour claim, which the "Open at two
        in the morning" panel below already makes at greater length. The band was
        making its own argument and then borrowing someone else's. */}
    <p class="text-lead mt-6 max-w-2xl">
      A staffed food pantry asks a student to walk through a door that announces
      why they are there. Pantry Point looks like every other vending machine on
      campus: the same credits at the same machine, and no way for anyone nearby
      to tell whether they were bought or granted.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="muted">
    <h2 class="text-display font-extrabold text-balance m-0">
      How the pilot ran
    </h2>

    {/* The tense is the point of this paragraph, and it is doing real work: it
        is the difference between a description and an offer. Everything below
        it is past tense and stays that way. */}
    <p class="text-lead mt-6 max-w-2xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
      History, not an offer — but it is the part that was actually tested, and a
      campus starting one has to settle the same questions.
    </p>

    <figure class="flow-figure m-0 mt-12">
      <img
        src="/figures/pantry-point-pilot.svg"
        alt=""
        width="1200"
        height="240"
        class="flow-diagram"
      />

      <figcaption class="flow-captions">
        {pilotSteps.map((step) => <p class="m-0">{step}</p>)}
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    {/* WAS A FEATURE PANEL ("DATA DRIVEN"), now a coda to the pilot.
        The two points of contact it describes are the survey and the scan —
        both of which are the mechanism above. Left in the grid it promised a
        capability independent of the thing that produced it, which is a promise
        this page cannot keep. */}
    <h3 class="panel-title pilot-coda mt-12">Two points of contact</h3>
    <p class="mt-4 m-0 max-w-3xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
      The survey and the scan were also the only things the pilot measured: what
      students said they needed, and then what they actually took, when, and
      where. A campus running this again gets the same two readings.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section>
    <h2 class="text-display font-extrabold text-balance m-0">
      Why the model works on a campus
    </h2>

    <ul class="panels panels--lead">
      {
        leadFeatures.map((feature) => (
          <li class="panel panel--lead">
            <h3 class="panel-title m-0">{feature.title}</h3>
            {feature.body.map((paragraph, i) => (
              <p class:list={['m-0', i === 0 ? 'mt-4' : 'mt-3']}>{paragraph}</p>
            ))}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <ul class="panels panels--rest">
      {
        restFeatures.map((feature) => (
          <li class="panel">
            <h3 class="panel-title m-0">{feature.title}</h3>
            {feature.body.map((paragraph) => (
              <p class="m-0 mt-3">{paragraph}</p>
            ))}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Section>

  {/* Muted, not inverse. The footer is brand green and full width, so an
      inverse band immediately above it merges into one undifferentiated green
      block and the closing call to action stops reading as its own thing. */}
  <Section tone="muted" size="tight">
    <h2 class="text-display font-extrabold text-balance m-0">
      Bring Pantry Point to your campus.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lead mt-6 max-w-2xl text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
      Tell us how your vending contracts and your dining program are set up, and
      we will tell you what running this would take.
    </p>
    {/* TODO(content): same open question as /app — confirm nucleus@ is still
        the right inbox for campus enquiries. Carried over from the printed
        one-pager and not verified. */}
    <p class="text-lead mt-6 m-0">
      <a
        href="mailto:nucleus@sharemeals.org"
        class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-brand-700)] underline"
        >nucleus@sharemeals.org</a
      >
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

{/*
  PRINT RULES SPECIFIC TO THIS SHEET.

  The bulk of the print layout is in `~/styles/one-pager.css`, imported at the
  top of this file and shared with /app. What is left here is what cannot be
  stated without naming this page: the proportions of its illustration, and the
  one band whose paragraphs need holding apart.

  `is:global` because the Section bands are rendered by a component and cannot
  be reached from a scoped block. Astro bundles CSS per page, so despite the
  name none of this is served on any other route.
*/}
<style is:global>
  /* Reserves the diagram's box before the file loads so the captions below do
     not jump. Matches the placeholder's viewBox; replacement art should keep
     roughly this ratio or this number needs to move with it. */
  .flow-diagram {
    aspect-ratio: 1200 / 240;
  }

  @media print {
    /* Capped by height, not width — the sheet is the one place this has a fixed
       budget, and the page-count guard in scripts/build-pdf.mjs is what proves
       the number still works. This drawing is a long horizontal pipeline, so at
       this height it is nowhere near the width of the printable box; height is
       the only constraint that binds. Which is also why the canvas is 5:1 and
       not the 1040:340 /app uses: at a fixed height, the only way this drawing
       gets bigger is by getting flatter. At 3.5:1 it rendered 312pt wide with
       the caption columns running past it into white space on both sides. */
    .flow-diagram {
      height: 78pt !important;
    }

    /* FOUR COLUMNS, not the shared sheet's two.
       This is a saving of about fifty points, but it is not only a saving: the
       diagram above is a left-to-right pipeline with four stages, so four
       captions in a row sit under the stages they describe. The two-column
       default is right for /app, whose drawing is a hub and whose captions have
       no left-to-right order to preserve. */
    .flow-captions {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)) !important;
    }

    /* Tailwind's `mt-12` is 3rem, and 48px of it survived into print because
       nothing in the sheet resets an arbitrary utility margin — it was the
       single largest piece of waste on this page, worth more than the diagram
       and the band spacing put together. Screen keeps the utility; paper does
       not. Worth checking for on any element that carries a spacing class into
       a print band. */
    .pilot-coda {
      margin-top: 8pt !important;
    }

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       SPACING, TIGHTER THAN THE SHARED SHEET.

       This page runs denser than /app's: five bands, one of them carrying a
       full-width figure, where /app has six shallower ones. It came in two
       pixels under a 960px budget on the first fit, which is not a fit — the
       note in scripts/build-pdf.mjs is about exactly this, and a margin inside
       the noise between how macOS and Linux rasterise the same webfont will
       pass here and spill onto a second page on CI.

       The remaining sixty points come out of the gaps rather than the copy.
       The sentences had already been cut twice by this point, and shaving a
       third time would have started removing the argument instead of the
       slack. Every number here is a reduction of a shared default, so raising
       one back is a local edit rather than a decision about the other sheet.
       ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    main section {
      margin-bottom: 4pt;
    }

    .panels {
      margin-top: 7pt !important;
    }

    .flow-figure {
      margin-top: 7pt !important;
    }

    .print-band-inverse {
      padding: 5pt 9pt !important;
    }

    /* The lead scale exists to set a paragraph apart from body copy, and 9.5pt
       against a 9pt body still does that on paper. 10.5pt was buying a
       distinction the sheet could no longer afford across three bands. */
    .text-lead {
      font-size: 9.5pt !important;
    }
  }
</style>
